## Context

Extend the proven adapter seam to the remaining members of Codex, Pi and Claude Code. Reuse library policy and common fixtures; do not introduce another store or mode matrix.

Design baseline: v7. v6 was merged by PR #903 and is frozen at `docs/2026-09-10-dragon-memory-design-v6.md`; v7 closes the acceptance review's four §12.1 conditions and settles the nomenclature, leaving the architecture and this ticket's scope unchanged. See `docs/2026-09-10-dragon-memory-design.md`, `docs/2026-09-10-dragon-memory-design-nomenclature.md` and `docs/2026-09-11-memory-implementation-plan.md`. This planning change implements nothing and closes no work.

## Relevant files

First adapter and shared tests from 0923; target runtime integration code/documentation; library CLI.

## Actions

1. Inspect primary current runtime interfaces and implement only event translation/delivery packaging.
2. Run the same canonical corpus/context fixtures and report tokenizer/capability differences.
3. Verify native-memory isolation and resume/child/compaction semantics per runtime. Mark unsupported boundaries explicitly; do not claim full equivalence.
4. Confirm bare-project browsing still works when each adapter is removed.

## Test

Actual smoke evidence on each target plus differential eligibility/lifecycle fixtures. A deliberately miswired delivery path must fail the smoke check rather than return empty success.

## Verification

- [ ] Run the focused fixtures and show they detect the relevant failure.
- [ ] Run applicable repository checks; record commands, results and limitations.
- [ ] Confirm the implementation matches this scope and v7, including explicit capability limits.

## Invariants

- Canonical bodies and provenance remain reviewable; memory cannot override decisions.
- No mandatory residency, automatic generalisation, new native-memory modes or policy duplication.
- Use the common library and normal Git integration; no runtime implementation is part of the planning PR.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] All three named targets have evidence-backed capability declarations.
- [ ] No policy fork, duplicate canonical writer or hidden native-memory budget.
- [ ] Runtime-specific limitations are visible and do not become fabricated guarantees.
